level0 - walkthrough
====================

Target: /home/user/level0/level0
        -rwsr-x---  owned by level1, setuid bit set
        setuid ELF 32-bit LSB, Intel 80386, statically linked, not stripped

Goal: read /home/user/level1/.pass

1. Recon
   $ file level0
   $ objdump -d level0 | sed -n '/<main>:/,/ret/p'

2. What main() does
   - argv[1] is passed to atoi().
   - The integer result is compared with 0x1a7 (= 423).
       8048ed4:  call   atoi
       8048ed9:  cmp    $0x1a7,%eax
       8048ede:  jne    <fail>
   - On a match it:
       * strdup("/bin/sh")
       * gid = getegid(); uid = geteuid();
       * setresgid(gid, gid, gid);   setresuid(uid, uid, uid);
         -> makes the (level1) effective id the real id too, so the
            privilege survives into the execed program.
       * execv("/bin/sh", {"/bin/sh", NULL});
   - On a mismatch it prints "No !".

3. Exploit
   There is no memory corruption here at all -- just satisfy the check by
   passing 423 as the argument. The setuid binary then hands us a shell
   running as level1.

       level0@RainFall:~$ ./level0 423
       $ id
       uid=2030(level1) gid=2020(level0) groups=2030(level1),...
       $ cat /home/user/level1/.pass

   Non-interactive form used to capture the flag:

       $ echo "cat /home/user/level1/.pass" | ./level0 423

